const pyqAnswers={
"Explain the basic structure and compilation process of C program.":"A C program is commonly organized into preprocessor directives, global declarations, the main() function, user-defined functions and executable statements. The compilation process is: preprocessing expands headers/macros; compilation translates C to assembly; assembly creates object code; linking combines object code with required libraries and produces the executable. Execution begins from main().",
"Describe the compilation process in C programming language.":"The source file first goes through preprocessing, where #include and #define directives are handled. The compiler translates the preprocessed C source into assembly code. The assembler converts assembly into object code. Finally, the linker combines object code with required library code and creates the executable file.",
"Briefly describe the basic structure and execution of a C Program.":"A basic C program contains header files, global declarations if required, main(), local declarations, executable statements and a return statement. Program execution starts from main() and proceeds according to the control statements used.",
"What are datatypes? What are the basic datatypes in C? Explain format specifier and memory requirement of each datatype?":"A data type tells the compiler what kind of value an object stores and what operations are appropriate. Common basic types are char, int, float, double and void. Typical format specifiers are %c, %d, %f and %lf respectively. Exact storage size is implementation-dependent, so sizeof(type) is the portable way to check it; common modern systems often use char=1, int=4, float=4 and double=8 bytes.",
"Briefly describe Data Types.":"Data types classify the values handled by a C program. The syllabus includes char, int, float, double, bool/_Bool and fixed-width integer types. The chosen type determines representation, range and the operations permitted on the value.",
"Briefly describe Variable and Constant.":"A variable is a named memory location whose stored value can change during execution, for example int marks = 80;. A constant is a value that should not change; const int MAX = 100 creates a typed const object, while #define MAX 100 creates a preprocessor macro.",
"Explain keywords & identifiers with example.":"Keywords are reserved words with predefined meaning, such as int, if, return and while. Identifiers are programmer-defined names for variables, functions, arrays etc. An identifier may contain letters, digits and underscore, cannot start with a digit, and cannot be a keyword. Example: int total_marks = 90; \u2014 total_marks is an identifier and int is a keyword.",
"Define Operator? Explain with example different types of operator arithmetic, logical, bitwise and ternary operator.":"An operator performs an operation on one or more operands. Arithmetic: +, -, *, /, %. Relational: <, >, ==, !=. Logical: &&, ||, !. Bitwise: &, |, ^, ~, <<, >>. Conditional/ternary: condition ? expr1 : expr2. Assignment operators include =, +=, -= etc., while ++ and -- increment/decrement values.",
"What is an operator. Explain different types of operators used in C programming.":"Operators are symbols that tell C to perform an operation. Major categories are arithmetic, relational, logical, bitwise, assignment, conditional, increment/decrement and special operators such as sizeof. Example: int max=(a>b)?a:b; uses the conditional operator.",
"Write short notes with suitable examples: Bit wise operator, Increment\u2013Decrement operator, Operator Precedence.":"Bitwise operators work on individual bits: & AND, | OR, ^ XOR, ~ NOT, << left shift and >> right shift. ++ increases a value by one and -- decreases it by one; each has prefix and postfix forms. Operator precedence determines which operators are evaluated first, while associativity resolves ties. Use parentheses when clarity matters.",
"What is type conversion? Explain two types of conversion with examples.":"Type conversion changes a value from one type to another. Implicit conversion is performed automatically, for example double x = 5 + 2.5; where 5 is converted for the expression. Explicit conversion uses a cast, for example float avg = (float)sum / n; which prevents integer division.",
"Predict the output of the program involving i/j*j and j/i*i.":"With int i=2 and j=3, integer division is used. i/j is 0, so (i/j)*j is 0. j/i is 1, so (j/i)*i is 2. Therefore the integer results are 0 and 2. The floating expressions depend on whether the source code correctly contains float operands; the supplied scan has formatting/OCR errors, so those should be traced from the exact code shown in class.",
"What is ternary operator? Design a flowchart and write a C program using ternary operator to check whether a given number is odd or even.":{text:"The conditional/ternary operator has the form condition ? expression1 : expression2. It selects expression1 when the condition is true, otherwise expression2. For odd/even, test n % 2 != 0.\n\nExam-ready program:",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n\n    printf(\"%s\", (n % 2 == 0) ? \"Even\" : \"Odd\");\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Any year is input through the keyboard. Write a program to determine whether the year is a leap year or not.":{text:"A leap year is divisible by 400, or divisible by 4 but not by 100. This condition avoids treating century years such as 1900 as leap years.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int year;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &year);\n\n    if (year % 400 == 0 || (year % 4 == 0 && year % 100 != 0))\n        printf(\"Leap year\");\n    else\n        printf(\"Not a leap year\");\n\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Find the output of the C code using a for loop, switch, continue and break.":"For i=2 the program prints A and continue skips to the next iteration of the for loop. For i=3, default prints C and return 0 terminates the program. Therefore the visible output is A followed by C, assuming the intended syntax in the scanned question is corrected.",
"Difference between while and do-while loop?":"while is entry-controlled: it checks the condition before executing the body, so the body may execute zero times. do-while is exit-controlled: it executes the body first and checks the condition afterward, so the body executes at least once. Syntax: while(condition){...}; versus do{...}while(condition);",
"Write difference between entry-controlled loop and exit controlled loop.":"In an entry-controlled loop, the condition is checked before the body, so zero executions are possible; while and for are examples. In an exit-controlled loop, the condition is checked after the body, so at least one execution occurs; do-while is the C example.",
"Why do we need a break and goto statement. Justify.":"break is used to terminate the nearest loop or switch immediately. goto transfers control to a labeled statement in the same function. break is common and structured; goto should be used sparingly because excessive jumps can make control flow difficult to understand and maintain.",
"Difference between flowchart and algorithm? Write an algorithm and flowchart to find the factorial of a number.":"An algorithm is a finite sequence of precise steps written in text; a flowchart represents the same logic graphically using standard symbols. Factorial algorithm: Start \u2192 input n \u2192 set fact=1 \u2192 repeat i=1 to n: fact=fact*i \u2192 output fact \u2192 End. In the flowchart use Start/End, Input/Output, Process and loop/decision symbols.",
"Define pseudocode. Write a pseudocode to find the area of triangle when we know the lengths of all three of its sides.":"Pseudocode is an informal, language-independent description of an algorithm. For sides a,b,c use Heron's formula: s=(a+b+c)/2 and area=sqrt(s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)). Steps: START; INPUT a,b,c; s=(a+b+c)/2; area=sqrt(s*(s-a)*(s-b)*(s-c)); OUTPUT area; END.",
"Write an algorithm and draw flowchart to find even numbers between 1 to 50.":"Algorithm: Start; set i=2; while i<=50, output i and increase i by 2; End. The flowchart uses Start \u2192 initialize i \u2192 decision i<=50? \u2192 output i \u2192 i=i+2 \u2192 back to decision \u2192 End.",
"What is recursion? Write a C program to find the factorial of number through recursion.":{text:"Recursion is a technique in which a function calls itself. A correct recursive function needs a base case and a recursive case. For factorial, 0!=1 and n!=n*(n-1)!.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nlong long factorial(int n)\n{\n    if (n <= 1)\n        return 1;\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);\n}\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    printf(\"%lld\", factorial(n));\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Define Recursion function? Write a C program to find the Fibonacci series using recursion.":{text:"In recursion, a function calls itself until a base case is reached. For Fibonacci, F(0)=0, F(1)=1 and F(n)=F(n-1)+F(n-2).",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint fib(int n)\n{\n    if (n <= 1)\n        return n;\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\n}\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int n, i;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)\n        printf(\"%d \", fib(i));\n    return 0;\n}"},
"What is call by Value and Call by reference? Write a C program to show working of Recursion.":{text:"In call by value, a copy of the argument is passed, so assigning to the parameter does not change the caller's variable. C does not have a separate reference-parameter mechanism; passing an address using a pointer is the usual way to let a function modify caller data. Recursion means a function calls itself with a smaller/subproblem input until a base case.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint sum(int n)\n{\n    if (n == 0) return 0;\n    return n + sum(n - 1);\n}\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int n;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    printf(\"%d\", sum(n));\n    return 0;\n}"},
"What is array? Explain the declaration and initialization of one dimensional and two dimensional arrays with an example.":"An array stores elements of the same type in contiguous memory and uses zero-based indexing. A 1D array can be declared as int a[5]={1,2,3,4,5};. A 2D array can be declared as int m[2][3]={{1,2,3},{4,5,6}};. Elements are accessed with indices such as a[2] or m[1][2].",
"What is array? Explain declaration and initialization of two-dimensional array? Write a C program to find matrix transpose.":{text:"A two-dimensional array is an array arranged as rows and columns. For a matrix a[r][c], transpose is formed by exchanging indices: t[j][i]=a[i][j].",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int a[10][10], t[10][10];\n    int r, c, i, j;\n\n    scanf(\"%d%d\", &r, &c);\n    for (i = 0; i < r; i++)\n        for (j = 0; j < c; j++)\n            scanf(\"%d\", &a[i][j]);\n\n    for (i = 0; i < r; i++)\n        for (j = 0; j < c; j++)\n            t[j][i] = a[i][j];\n\n    for (i = 0; i < c; i++) {\n        for (j = 0; j < r; j++)\n            printf(\"%d \", t[i][j]);\n        printf(\"\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Define array. Write a program to print the Maximum and Minimum element in an array.":{text:"Read n elements, initialize both minimum and maximum from the first element, then scan the remaining elements and update each when a smaller/larger value is found.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int a[100], n, i, min, max;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) scanf(\"%d\", &a[i]);\n\n    min = max = a[0];\n    for (i = 1; i < n; i++) {\n        if (a[i] < min) min = a[i];\n        if (a[i] > max) max = a[i];\n    }\n    printf(\"Maximum = %d\\nMinimum = %d\", max, min);\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Write a C program to print Fibonacci Series up to n numbers.":{text:"Start with 0 and 1; each next term is the sum of the previous two terms. Print n terms.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int n, i;\n    long long a = 0, b = 1, next;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        printf(\"%lld \", a);\n        next = a + b;\n        a = b;\n        b = next;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Write two different C functions to compute area and perimeter of a triangle.":{text:"The perimeter is a+b+c. For a valid triangle, Heron's formula gives area=sqrt(s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)), where s=(a+b+c)/2.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <math.h>\n\ndouble perimeter(double a, double b, double c) { return a + b + c; }\ndouble area(double a, double b, double c) {\n    double s = (a + b + c) / 2.0;\n    return sqrt(s * (s-a) * (s-b) * (s-c));\n}\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    double a,b,c;\n    scanf(\"%lf%lf%lf\", &a,&b,&c);\n    printf(\"Perimeter = %.2f\\nArea = %.2f\", perimeter(a,b,c), area(a,b,c));\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Write a program to check whether a string is a palindrome or not.":{text:"A palindrome reads identically from both ends. Compare matching characters from the first and last positions and stop on a mismatch.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    char s[100];\n    int i, j, ok=1;\n    fgets(s, sizeof s, stdin);\n    s[strcspn(s,\"\\n\")]='\\0';\n    for(i=0, j=(int)strlen(s)-1; i<j; i++,j--)\n        if(s[i]!=s[j]) { ok=0; break; }\n    printf(ok ? \"Palindrome\" : \"Not palindrome\");\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Write a C program to check if a number has three consecutive 5s. If yes, print YES, else print NO.":{text:"Check the decimal digits from right to left, counting consecutive digits equal to 5. If the count reaches 3, print YES.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    long long n;\n    int count = 0, found = 0;\n    scanf(\"%lld\", &n);\n\n    if (n < 0) n = -n;\n    while (n > 0) {\n        if (n % 10 == 5) {\n            count++;\n            if (count == 3) { found = 1; break; }\n        } else {\n            count = 0;\n        }\n        n /= 10;\n    }\n    printf(found ? \"YES\" : \"NO\");\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Write a program to eliminate multiple spaces from a sentence and make it single.":{text:"Read the sentence character by character. When a space is encountered, output one space only if the previous output was not already a space. This removes runs of multiple spaces.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    char s[500];\n    int i, space = 0;\n\n    fgets(s, sizeof s, stdin);\n    for (i = 0; s[i] != '\\0'; i++) {\n        if (s[i] == ' ') {\n            if (!space) putchar(' ');\n            space = 1;\n        } else {\n            putchar(s[i]);\n            space = 0;\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"},
"What do you mean by Sorting? Write a program to sort a list of n numbers in descending order using bubble sort.":{text:"Bubble sort repeatedly compares adjacent elements and swaps them when they are in the wrong order. For descending order, swap when a[j] < a[j+1]. After each pass, the smallest remaining value moves toward the end.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int a[100], n, i, j, temp;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (i=0; i<n; i++) scanf(\"%d\", &a[i]);\n\n    for (i=0; i<n-1; i++)\n        for (j=0; j<n-1-i; j++)\n            if (a[j] < a[j+1]) {\n                temp=a[j]; a[j]=a[j+1]; a[j+1]=temp;\n            }\n\n    for (i=0; i<n; i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Define Selection Sort? Sort the given elements using selection sort: 77,33,44,11,88,22,66,55.":"For ascending selection sort, find the smallest remaining value and place it at the current position. The passes produce: 11,33,44,77,88,22,66,55 \u2192 11,22,44,77,88,33,66,55 \u2192 11,22,33,77,88,44,66,55 \u2192 11,22,33,44,88,77,66,55 \u2192 11,22,33,44,55,77,66,88 \u2192 11,22,33,44,55,66,77,88. Final ascending order: 11,22,33,44,55,66,77,88.",
"Write a program to sort n number of elements in descending order using selection sort.":{text:"For every position, select the largest remaining element and swap it into that position. This directly produces descending order.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int a[100], n, i, j, max, temp;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for(i=0;i<n;i++) scanf(\"%d\", &a[i]);\n    for(i=0;i<n-1;i++) {\n        max=i;\n        for(j=i+1;j<n;j++)\n            if(a[j]>a[max]) max=j;\n        temp=a[i]; a[i]=a[max]; a[max]=temp;\n    }\n    for(i=0;i<n;i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    return 0;\n}"},
"What is insertion sort? Write a C program to implement insertion sort.":{text:"Insertion sort maintains a sorted left portion. For each key, shift larger elements one position right and insert the key into its correct position.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int a[100], n, i, j, key;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (i=0; i<n; i++) scanf(\"%d\", &a[i]);\n\n    for (i=1; i<n; i++) {\n        key=a[i];\n        j=i-1;\n        while (j>=0 && a[j]>key) {\n            a[j+1]=a[j];\n            j--;\n        }\n        a[j+1]=key;\n    }\n    for (i=0; i<n; i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    return 0;\n}"},
"What is Binary search? Search item 15 from 2,5,8,10,15,24,38,59,75,90 using binary search.":"The data are sorted. With zero-based indices low=0 and high=9, mid=(0+9)/2=4. The element at index 4 is 15, so 15 is found at the 5th position after one comparison.",
"Write an algorithm and develop a C program to search an integer from N numbers using binary searching technique.":{text:"Binary search requires sorted data. Compare the key with the middle element; if the key is larger, move low to mid+1, otherwise move high to mid-1. Repeat until found or low exceeds high.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int a[100], n, key, low, high, mid, found=-1, i;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for (i=0; i<n; i++) scanf(\"%d\", &a[i]);\n    scanf(\"%d\", &key);\n\n    low=0; high=n-1;\n    while (low<=high) {\n        mid=low+(high-low)/2;\n        if (a[mid]==key) { found=mid; break; }\n        if (a[mid]<key) low=mid+1;\n        else high=mid-1;\n    }\n    if (found!=-1) printf(\"Found at index %d\", found);\n    else printf(\"Not found\");\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Write an algorithm for binary search.":"Algorithm: 1 Start. 2 Set low=0, high=n-1. 3 While low<=high, calculate mid=low+(high-low)/2. 4 If a[mid]==key, report found. 5 If a[mid]<key, set low=mid+1; otherwise set high=mid-1. 6 If the loop ends, report not found. 7 Stop.",
"Explain any one searching algorithm with example.":"Linear search checks elements sequentially until the key is found or the array ends. Example: searching 30 in [10,20,30,40] checks 10, then 20, then 30 and stops. Its worst-case time is O(n) and it does not require sorted data.",
"What is string? Write a program to find the length of a string?":{text:"A C string is a sequence of characters terminated by the null character '\\0'. The standard library function strlen() returns the number of characters before '\\0'.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    char s[100];\n    fgets(s, sizeof s, stdin);\n    s[strcspn(s, \"\\n\")] = '\\0';\n    printf(\"Length = %zu\", strlen(s));\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Define string? How it can be declared and initialized? Explain any five-string function with example.":"A string is a char array terminated by '\\0'. It may be declared as char s[20]; or initialized as char s[]=\"Hello\";. Five common functions are strlen(s) for length, strcpy(d,s) for copying, strcat(d,s) for concatenation, strcmp(a,b) for comparison and strchr(s,c) for finding a character. Always ensure destination capacity is sufficient.",
"Explain the command line arguments in C with the help of example.":{text:"Command-line arguments are received through int argc and char *argv[]. argc stores the number of arguments, while argv is an array of strings containing them. argv[0] is normally the program name. Always check argc before accessing argv[1] etc.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(int argc, char *argv[])\n{\n    int i;\n    for (i=0; i<argc; i++)\n        printf(\"argv[%d] = %s\\n\", i, argv[i]);\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Write an algorithm to find the square root of an inputted number.":"The supplied PYQ asks for a square-root algorithm but does not specify a numerical method. Therefore the exact method taught by your instructor should be used. If Newton-Raphson is the prescribed method, define x0>0 and repeatedly use x(n+1)=0.5*(x(n)+N/x(n)) until the required accuracy is reached.",
"Explain the concept of Dynamic memory allocation with an example.":{text:"Dynamic memory allocation obtains memory during runtime. malloc allocates uninitialized bytes, calloc allocates zero-initialized elements, realloc changes an allocation's size, and free releases it. The pointer returned by malloc/calloc should be checked before use.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int n, i;\n    int *a;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    a = malloc((size_t)n * sizeof *a);\n    if (a == NULL) return 1;\n    for (i=0; i<n; i++) scanf(\"%d\", &a[i]);\n    for (i=0; i<n; i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    free(a);\n    return 0;\n}"},
"What is the need of file handling? Write a program to count the no of words and characters in a file.":{text:"File handling allows data to be stored and retrieved persistently instead of only during program execution. A simple word counter treats a transition from whitespace to a non-whitespace character as the start of a word; every non-whitespace character contributes to the character count.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <ctype.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    FILE *fp;\n    int ch, in_word=0;\n    long words=0, chars=0;\n\n    fp=fopen(\"input.txt\",\"r\");\n    if(fp==NULL) return 1;\n    while((ch=fgetc(fp)) != EOF) {\n        chars++;\n        if(isspace((unsigned char)ch)) in_word=0;\n        else if(!in_word) { words++; in_word=1; }\n    }\n    fclose(fp);\n    printf(\"Words = %ld\\nCharacters = %ld\", words, chars);\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Define a structure. WAP in C to create an array of structures and print the details.":{text:"A structure is a user-defined type that groups related members of different types under one name. An array of structures stores multiple records of the same structure type.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nstruct Student {\n    int roll;\n    char name[30];\n    float marks;\n};\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    struct Student s[5];\n    int i;\n    for(i=0;i<5;i++)\n        scanf(\"%d %29s %f\", &s[i].roll, s[i].name, &s[i].marks);\n    for(i=0;i<5;i++)\n        printf(\"%d %s %.2f\\n\", s[i].roll, s[i].name, s[i].marks);\n    return 0;\n}"},
"What is a header file. Explain two library functions of the following header files:":"A header file contains declarations and macros that can be shared by source files. Examples: stdlib.h provides malloc() and free(); string.h provides strlen() and strcpy(); ctype.h provides isdigit() and toupper(). The functions become available after including the appropriate header.",
"Write short description and function used in stdio.h, math.h and string.h.":"stdio.h provides input/output facilities such as printf(), scanf(), getchar(), putchar(), fgets() and fputs(). math.h provides mathematical functions such as sqrt(), pow() and fabs(). string.h provides string/memory functions such as strlen(), strcpy(), strcat() and strcmp(). Include the header before using its declarations.",
"What is the difference between #include <filename> and #include \"filename\"?":"For #include <filename>, the implementation normally searches the configured system/include directories. For #include \"filename\", the implementation normally searches the current/project include location first and then configured include paths. Exact search order is implementation-defined, but the practical distinction is system-style versus local/project-style headers.",
"What is a pointer? Write a C program to find the sum and mean of all elements in an array using pointer.":{text:"A pointer stores the address of another object. The * operator dereferences the pointer to access the pointed value. For an array, a pointer can traverse elements using p+i.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    int a[100], n, i;\n    int *p;\n    long sum=0;\n    scanf(\"%d\", &n);\n    for(i=0;i<n;i++) scanf(\"%d\", &a[i]);\n    p=a;\n    for(i=0;i<n;i++) sum += *(p+i);\n    printf(\"Sum = %ld\\nMean = %.2f\", sum, (double)sum/n);\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Write a C program to count the number of words and characters in a file.":{text:"Open the file in read mode, process each character, count every non-whitespace character and count a word whenever whitespace changes to a non-whitespace character.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <ctype.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    FILE *fp=fopen(\"input.txt\",\"r\");\n    int ch,in_word=0; long words=0,chars=0;\n    if(!fp) return 1;\n    while((ch=fgetc(fp))!=EOF){\n        chars++;\n        if(isspace((unsigned char)ch)) in_word=0;\n        else if(!in_word){words++;in_word=1;}\n    }\n    fclose(fp);\n    printf(\"Words=%ld Characters=%ld\",words,chars);\n    return 0;\n}"},
"Define a structure data type named Time containing 3 members called hr, min and sec. Develop a program that would assign values to the individual members and display the time in the form 16:40:30 and also add structure t1 and t2.":{text:"Define struct Time with hr, min and sec. Add seconds and minutes with carry: seconds >=60 becomes minutes+1, minutes>=60 becomes hours+1. Normalize the result before printing.",code:"#include <stdio.h>\n\nstruct Time { int hr, min, sec; };\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    struct Time t1,t2,sum;\n    scanf(\"%d%d%d\",&t1.hr,&t1.min,&t1.sec);\n    scanf(\"%d%d%d\",&t2.hr,&t2.min,&t2.sec);\n    sum.sec=t1.sec+t2.sec;\n    sum.min=t1.min+t2.min+sum.sec/60;\n    sum.sec%=60;\n    sum.hr=t1.hr+t2.hr+sum.min/60;\n    sum.min%=60;\n    printf(\"%02d:%02d:%02d\",sum.hr,sum.min,sum.sec);\n    return 0;\n}"},
"What is a Function in C? Explain the library and user defined functions in C.":"A function is a named block of code that performs a specific task. Library functions are supplied by the C library, such as printf(), scanf(), strlen() and sqrt(). User-defined functions are written by the programmer and have a declaration/prototype, definition and call. Functions improve modularity, reuse and readability.",
"What are actual parameters and formal parameters? Illustrate with example.":{text:"Actual parameters (arguments) are the values/expressions supplied at the function call. Formal parameters are the variables listed in the function definition that receive those values. Example: in add(2,3), 2 and 3 are actual arguments; in int add(int a,int b), a and b are formal parameters.",code:"int add(int a, int b)\n{\n    return a + b;\n}\n\nint result = add(2, 3);"},
"Differentiate between put(), putchar() and puts().":"The standard C function is putchar(), which writes one character. puts() writes a string and automatically appends a newline. The spelling put() is not a standard C output function; if the paper intended putchar(), distinguish it from puts() as above.",
"Find the output of the program using strcpy(), strcat(), strrev() and strlen().":"The supplied paper has OCR/syntax errors and uses strrev(), which is not part of the ISO C standard. The intended operations are: strcat(str1,str2) makes HelloWorld; strcpy copies that result to str2; strrev reverses str1; strlen counts characters in str2. For a strict exam answer, reproduce the intended output only after checking the exact classroom/compiler version."
};
const state={unit:'all',query:'',important:false,answers:true,current:0,done:new Set(JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('cGuideDone')||'[]'))};
const $=s=>document.querySelector(s); const list=$('#topicList'), view=$('#topicView');
function save(){localStorage.setItem('cGuideDone',JSON.stringify([...state.done]));}
function filtered(){return topics.map((t,i)=>({...t,i})).filter(x=>(state.unit==='all'||String(x.u)===state.unit)&&(!state.important||x.tags.includes('high'))&&(x.t.toLowerCase().includes(state.query.toLowerCase())||x.intro.toLowerCase().includes(state.query.toLowerCase())||(pyqMap[x.t]||[]).some(q=>q[1].toLowerCase().includes(state.query.toLowerCase()))))}
function renderNav(){const arr=filtered();list.innerHTML=arr.length?arr.map(x=>`<button class="topic-link ${state.done.has(x.i)?'done':''} ${x.i===state.current?'active':''}" data-id="${x.i}"><span class="check"></span><span class="num">${String(x.i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><span>${x.t}</span></button>`).join(''):`<div class="empty">No matching topics.</div>`;}
function esc(s){return s.replace(/[&<>]/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;'}[c]));}
function renderTopic(){const t=topics[state.current]; if(!t)return; const qs=pyqMap[t.t]||[]; const pyqHtml=qs.length?`<div class="card pyq-card"><div class="pyq-title"><h3>🔥 PYQs for this topic</h3><span>${qs.length} question${qs.length>1?'s':''}</span></div><div class="pyq-list">${qs.map((q,i)=>{const ans=pyqAnswers[q[1]]||'Answer: The uploaded paper provides the question but not an official answer key; use the topic explanation and write the complete exam method/program where applicable.'; const answerHtml=typeof ans==='object'?`<strong>Answer / exam-ready solution:</strong><p>${esc(ans.text)}</p>${ans.code?`<div class="code pyq-code"><button class="copy">Copy</button><pre>${esc(ans.code)}</pre></div>`:''}`:`<strong>Answer / exam-ready solution:</strong> ${esc(ans)}`; return `<div class="pyq-item"><div class="pyq-year">${q[0]}</div><div class="pyq-q">${esc(q[1])}</div><div class="pyq-answer answer ${state.answers?'':'hidden'}">${answerHtml}</div></div>`}).join('')}</div></div>`:`<div class="card pyq-card muted-pyq"><div class="pyq-title"><h3>🔥 PYQs for this topic</h3></div><p>No direct PYQ for this exact topic was found in the uploaded ES-102 papers. I haven't invented one.</p></div>`; view.innerHTML=`<article class="topic" id="topic-${state.current}"><div class="topic-head"><div class="topic-num">${String(state.current+1).padStart(2,'0')}</div><div><h2>${t.t}</h2><div class="topic-meta">${t.u===1?'<span class="tag">Unit I</span>':'<span class="tag">Unit II</span>'}${t.tags.includes('high')?'<span class="tag high">Very important</span>':''}${t.tags.includes('pyq')?'<span class="tag pyq">PYQ</span>':''}${t.tags.includes('program')?'<span class="tag program">Program</span>':''}</div></div></div><div class="card"><h3>What you need to know</h3><p>${t.intro}</p><div class="answer ${state.answers?'':'hidden'}"><p>${t.body}</p></div></div>${t.code?`<div class="card"><h3>Exam-ready code / pattern</h3><div class="code"><button class="copy">Copy</button><pre>${esc(t.code)}</pre></div></div>`:''}${t.exam?`<div class="card"><h3>Exam focus</h3><p>${t.exam}</p></div>`:''}${pyqHtml}<div class="complete-row"><button class="complete-btn ${state.done.has(state.current)?'done':''}" id="completeBtn">${state.done.has(state.current)?'✓ Completed':'Mark complete'}</button><span class="next">${state.current<topics.length-1?'Next topic →':''}</span></div></article>`; view.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'start'}); updateProgress();}
function updateProgress(){const n=state.done.size,p=topics.length?Math.round(n/topics.length*100):0;$('#progressText').textContent=`${n} / ${topics.length} topics done`,$('#progressPct').textContent=p+'%',$('#progressBar').style.width=p+'%';}
function render(){renderNav();renderTopic();}
list.addEventListener('click',e=>{const b=e.target.closest('[data-id]');if(!b)return;state.current=+b.dataset.id;render();if(innerWidth<=820)$('#sidebar').classList.remove('open');});
$('#search').addEventListener('input',e=>{state.query=e.target.value;renderNav()});
document.querySelectorAll('.unit-btn').forEach(b=>b.addEventListener('click',()=>{document.querySelectorAll('.unit-btn').forEach(x=>x.classList.remove('active'));b.classList.add('active');state.unit=b.dataset.unit;renderNav()}));
$('#importantToggle').addEventListener('click',()=>{state.important=!state.important;$('#importantToggle').classList.toggle('active',state.important);renderNav()});
$('#answersToggle').addEventListener('click',()=>{state.answers=!state.answers;$('#answersToggle').textContent='Answers: '+(state.answers?'ON':'OFF');renderTopic()});
$('#completeBtn')?.addEventListener('click',()=>{});
document.addEventListener('click',e=>{if(e.target.id==='completeBtn'){const i=state.current;state.done.has(i)?state.done.delete(i):state.done.add(i);save();render();showToast(state.done.has(i)?'Topic completed ✓':'Marked incomplete');} if(e.target.id==='startBtn'){state.current=0;render();} if(e.target.classList.contains('copy')){navigator.clipboard?.writeText(e.target.nextElementSibling.textContent);showToast('Code copied');}});
$('#topicsBtn').addEventListener('click',()=>$('#sidebar').classList.add('open'));$('#closeTopics').addEventListener('click',()=>$('#sidebar').classList.remove('open'));
function showToast(msg){const t=$('#toast');t.textContent=msg;t.classList.add('show');setTimeout(()=>t.classList.remove('show'),1300)}
render();
